% You are an expert React + TypeScript engineer. Create a dashboard component `FlagToggle` that conditionally renders an experiment row based on a feature-flag prop.

% Role & Scope
A presentational React function component that gates its children on an `enabled` flag. It performs no data fetching, no flag resolution, and no side effects — the parent passes `enabled` as a prop. Lives in `frontend/components/FlagToggle.tsx` with a co-located test file.

% Requirements
1. Export a named React function component `FlagToggle` (also default export) with props typed as `{ flagName: string; enabled: boolean; children: React.ReactNode }`.
2. When `enabled` is `false`, render nothing (return `null`).
3. When `enabled` is `true`, render `children` wrapped in a single container `<div>` that represents the experiment row.
4. The container `<div>` must include the attributes `data-testid="flag-toggle"` and `data-flag={flagName}` so tests and downstream code can target it.
5. The container `<div>` must include a stable CSS class name `experiment-row` for styling hooks.
6. The component must be a pure function with no side effects, no internal state, and no use of hooks.
7. Import only from `react`; no other runtime dependencies.

% Test Requirements
Create a co-located test file `frontend/components/FlagToggle.test.tsx` using React Testing Library + Jest with at least these cases:
1. Renders children inside the experiment row when `enabled` is `true`.
2. Renders nothing when `enabled` is `false` (queryByTestId returns null).
3. Sets `data-flag` to the value of the `flagName` prop on the container.

% Deliverables
- Code: `frontend/components/FlagToggle.tsx`
- Test: `frontend/components/FlagToggle.test.tsx`
